Ans.** \(\mathbf{(d)}:\) We have, \(2x = - 1 + i\sqrt{3}\)\
\[{\Rightarrow x = - \frac{1}{2} + \frac{i\sqrt{3}}{2} = \omega
}{\therefore x^{2} = \omega^{2}
}{\left( 1 - \omega^{2} + \omega \right)^{6} - \left( 1 - \omega + \omega^{2} \right)^{6}
}{\left( - \omega^{2} - \omega^{2} \right)^{6} - ( - \omega - \omega)^{6}
}{2^{6}\left\lbrack \omega^{12} - \omega^{6} \right\rbrack = 2^{6}\lbrack 1 - 1\rbrack
}{2^{6} \times 0 = 0}\]

Ans. (c)** : We have,

\(\begin{matrix}
cotx & \  = \sqrt{3} \\
\therefore\ cotx & \  = cot\frac{\pi}{6} \\
x & \  = \frac{\pi}{6}
\end{matrix}\)

For principal solution \(x \in (0,2\pi)\)

\(\begin{matrix}
\therefore\ cotx & \  = cot\left( \pi + \frac{\pi}{6} \right) \\
cotx & \  = cot\left( \frac{7\pi}{6} \right) \\
x & \  = \frac{7\pi}{6}
\end{matrix}\)

Hence, principal solution of \(cotx = \sqrt{3}\) is \(\left( \frac{\pi}{6},\frac{7\pi}{6} \right)\)

** Since, slope of line \(xcos\theta + ysin\theta = 2\) is \(- cot\theta\) and slope of line \(x - y = 3\) is 1 . Also, these lines are perpendicular to each other.

\(\begin{matrix}
 & \ \therefore\ ( - cot\theta)(1) = - 1 \\
 & \  \Rightarrow cot\theta = 1 = cot\frac{\pi}{4} \Rightarrow \theta = \frac{\pi}{4}
\end{matrix}\)

